  "headline": "The bond market is doing the tightening: an inflation repricing, not a growth scare",
  "body": [
    "Treasury yields at fresh 52-week highs, a firm dollar and a falling gold price read as one thing: markets are pricing a hotter inflation path and a tighter rate response while growth holds. The 10-year sits at 5.26%, up 29 basis points this week and 54 over the month, outpacing the 30-year's 39-basis-point monthly rise. That has flattened the 30y–10y spread by 15 basis points to 34 — a bear flattening, the shape the curve takes when the expected policy-rate path, not long-run growth, is being marked up.",
    "The inflation impulse is energy: Brent is up 8% over the month and 58% year-to-date in a choppy volatility regime. But this is not stagflation. Copper sits at 88% of its 52-week range, up 18% year-to-date, and the copper/gold ratio has climbed 9% over the month; bitcoin buys 12% more gold than a month ago. Industrial metal and the most liquidity-sensitive asset outrunning gold is a growth-and-risk-appetite signal. Gold, down 7% over the month against a dollar index up 1.7%, is trading as a rate-sensitive asset, not a haven.",
    "The tension is this week: yields jumped 29 basis points and everything dollar-priced fell — gold 4%, copper 4%, bitcoin 3.5% — with the dollar's 0.8% gain explaining only a fraction. The month and quarter carry more weight than five sessions, but the week marks where higher yields start to bite risk appetite."
  ],
